Peak RSS, three-arm decomposition — and a CORRECTION to the still numbers in
benchmarks/mem_2026-09-02.meta, which were inflated by the harness process.

date:   2026-09-02 (same day, later than mem_2026-09-02.*)
host:   mac — Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, 24 GiB, aarch64-darwin. macOS max RSS.
commit: 3fe2e2f95686
command: MEM_REPS=7 MEM_QP=40 MEM_SIZES="64 128 256 512 1024 1536 2048" \
         MEM_RS_BIN=<target/release/examples/perf_encode> tools/mem_gate.sh <preset>
         with (MEM_FRAMES=1), (MEM_FRAMES=1 MEM_VIDEO=1) and (MEM_FRAMES=2).
data:   benchmarks/mem_arms_2026-09-02.tsv (42 cells, median of 7 runs each)

=================== CORRECTION: ~14.5 MiB WAS THE HARNESS ====================
`mem_2026-09-02.{tsv,meta}` measured the port through `identity_run`. Measured
here, same encoder library, same cell, same day, only the harness binary
changed:

  gradient 2048x2048 preset 13, port peak RSS      C peak RSS   ratio
    identity_run, qp 32                102,832 KiB   87,520 KiB  1.17x
    perf_encode,  qp 32                 88,480 KiB   87,520 KiB  1.01x
    identity_run, qp 40                102,384 KiB   87,440 KiB  1.17x
    perf_encode,  qp 40                 87,936 KiB   87,440 KiB  1.01x

The variable is the BINARY, not the quantizer: ~14.5 MiB at 4 MP (3.6 MiB/MP)
is `identity_run`'s own buffers, not the encoder's. **The STILL path's real
memory ratio is 1.01-1.04x C, not the 1.17-1.18x that record reports.**

The INTER numbers in that record are NOT affected in the same way: at
2048x2048 p13 the inter arm measures 172,800 KiB through identity_run (qp 32)
and 177,459 KiB through perf_encode (qp 40) — the harness's extra buffers sit
BELOW the encoder's own peak once an inter frame is in flight, so they do not
add to it. Both records agree the inter path is ~1.55-1.60x at 4 MP.

======================= THE THREE ARMS, alpha + beta*px ======================
Same decomposition the CPU record uses (benchmarks/perf_inter_attrib_2026-09-02),
so the two can be read together.

  arm            range      port MiB fixed  MiB/MP   C fixed  MiB/MP  slope ratio
  still    p8    64..2048        4.38        20.67     6.32    19.88     1.04
  videokey p8    64..2048        4.59        33.37     8.13    41.11     0.81
  inter    p8    64..512         5.44        50.99     8.29    50.95     1.00
  still    p13   64..2048        4.21        20.61     6.12    19.90     1.04
  videokey p13   64..2048        4.69        25.22     6.67    22.27     1.13
  inter    p13   64..2048        5.18        42.04     7.19    25.46     1.65
(p8's inter arm stops at 512x512: the port REFUSES frame 1 at >= 576x576.)

Per-cell ratios, preset 13 (the only preset whose inter arm reaches 4 MP):

  size      still    videokey   inter
  64x64     0.61x     0.67x     0.81x
  256x256   0.73x     0.75x     0.93x
  512x512   0.81x     0.86x     1.14x
  1024x1024 1.01x     1.09x     1.39x
  1536x1536 1.00x     1.06x     1.54x
  2048x2048 1.01x     1.11x     1.60x

**STILL AND VIDEOKEY BOTH MEET THE 25 % MEMORY GOAL AT EVERY SIZE MEASURED.**
The still arm is at parity from 1 MP up (1.00-1.01x) and lighter below it; the
video-mode KEY frame adds little (1.11x at 4 MP, and at preset 8 the port is
LIGHTER than C for it, 0.81x slope). **The whole memory gap is the INTER
FRAME.**

======================= WHERE THE INTER MEMORY GOES =========================
By subtraction of measured arms — what one inter frame ADDS to a video-mode
key frame's peak:

  preset 13    port MiB   C MiB   ratio
  256x256         2.2       0.8    2.8x
  512x512         5.0       1.5    3.3x
  1024x1024      14.4       3.9    3.7x
  1536x1536      39.1       7.8    5.0x
  2048x2048      68.1      13.2    5.2x

At 4 MP that is 54.9 MiB of the arms' 64.9 MiB total gap — **85 % of the inter
path's memory excess is the inter frame's own footprint.** Per megapixel the
port adds 17.0 MiB/MP for one reference frame and C adds 3.3. One 8-bit 4:2:0
reference is 1.43 MiB/MP of pixels, so C carries ~2.3x the raw reference and
the port ~11.9x.

The ratio also GROWS with size (2.8x at 0.06 MP to 5.2x at 4 MP), so it is not
one oversized fixed structure; it is per-pixel state whose count or width scales
with the frame. The obvious candidates — the HME pyramid levels the CPU record
shows the port building (`inter_me::hme::hme_level_{0,1}`, `prehme_core`), the
reference planes themselves, and any per-SB inter scratch — are NOT separated
here. Separating them needs an allocation trace (heaptrack on Linux, or
MallocStackLogging + malloc_history on macOS), which this record does NOT have.
Do not attribute the 17.0 MiB/MP to a specific structure from this table.

=========================== NOT MEASURED ===================================
* Any inter cell above 512x512 that is BYTE-IDENTICAL to C (the frontier ends
  at 256x256), so the 1.39-1.60x cells compare two encoders making different
  decisions. They are reported as two absolute numbers and their quotient.
* More than one inter frame (the port refuses frame 2).
* Allocation-level attribution (see above). RSS is a ceiling on what the process
  touched, not an allocator trace.
* 10-bit, real content, non-square dims, Linux/glibc.
